W: 1. It's far away I am today from scenes I roamed a boy.
W: And long ago the hour I know I first saw Illinois.
W: But time nor tide nor waters wide could wean my heart away.
W: For ever true it flies to you, my dear old Galway Bay.
W:
W: 2. Oh, grey and bleak, by shore and creek, the rugged rocks abound,
W: But sweet and green the grass between, as grows on Irish ground,
W: So friendship fond, all wealth abound, and love that lives always,
W: Bless each poor home beside your foam, my dear old Galway Bay.
W:
W: 3. Had I youth's blood and hopeful mood and heart of fire once more,
W: For all the gold the world might hold I′d never leave your shore,
W: I'd be content with whatever God sent with neighbours old and gray,
W: And I'd lay my bones, ′neath churchyard stones, beside you, Galway Bay.
W:
W: 4. The blessing of a poor old man be with you night and day,
W: The blessing of a lonely man whose heart will soon be clay;
W: It's all the Heaven I'll ask of God upon my dying day,
W: My soul to soar for ever more above you, Galway Bay.
